# gamescope for ARM handhelds: the OpenGamingCollective fork plus armada's 13
# handheld patches -- EDID synthesis for edidless internal panels, gamma22 HDR
# composition without hardware colour management, arm64 Steam night mode,
# drm-lease. Those patches are the reason this package exists.
#
# The BASE is not tracked: the PKGBUILD builds the fork at the ref Terra pins,
# which is what the patches are maintained against. Pinning a commit of our own
# would drift away from them rather than with them; if the fork moves ahead,
# prepare() fails loudly.
#
# ── why this is no longer TRACK=oci ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# armada-os/armada-packages was ARCHIVED on 2026-09-18 and its contents moved
# into armada-os/armada under packages/. The OCI artifacts went with it: the new
# repo tags package images by CONTENT HASH and publishes no `latest`, so there is
# no artifact to resolve a version from, no monotonic tag to compare, and -- the
# part that actually mattered -- no sha embedded in a tag to read the patch set
# at. The old ghcr.io/armada-os/armada-packages/* images still resolve, frozen,
# and would have gone on reporting "up to date" forever.
#
# So the COMMIT is the release now. Everything still comes off one ref, which is
# the invariant worth keeping: the patch set and the source ref below are both
# read at CURRENT, and can never be resolved from two different moments.
#
# TRACK_PATHS is what THIS package consumes, not what armada considers a rebuild.
# Their build.sh and PATCHES.md are deliberately absent -- we do not run their
# build. TERRA.env is listed separately BECAUSE it sits outside
# packages/gamescope/: it names the source ref, it moves on its own, and watching
# only the package directory would miss it silently. That is the same class of
# failure as the moving-branch bug described below, and it is worth one extra
# request per poll to make it impossible.
TRACK=github-paths
GITHUB_REPO=armada-os/armada
TRACK_PATHS="packages/gamescope/patches packages/TERRA.env"

# The patch set, read at the tracked commit. PATCHES_REF is unset: CURRENT is
# already the git ref, which is the default.
PATCHES_REPO=armada-os/armada
PATCHES_PATH=packages/gamescope

CURRENT=a0bd432ffc5768d2fbaa752892c66f7e7bab9d8a

# pkgver is <commit date>.<short sha>, which is the shape the published artifact
# tag had (20260917.c8c19ee8 was the last one). Keeping the shape is not
# sentiment: pkgver has to sort FORWARD across the tracking change or every
# device's `pacman -Syu` reads the new package as a downgrade and declines it.
# A date-led version is the only form that keeps sorting past the old tags, and
# it still answers what the old one did -- `pacman -Q gamescope` names exactly
# which upstream commit the installed build came from.
VERSION_FROM=commit-date

# ── the source ref, derived from the SAME commit as the patches ─────────────
#
# armada packages from Terra rather than from a tree of their own: they clone
# terrapkg at a pinned TERRA_COMMIT and inject the patches above into Terra's
# spec, and it is that spec which names the upstream ref. So the ref their
# patches are written against is two hops from what we track:
#
#   CURRENT -> packages/TERRA.env TERRA_COMMIT -> terra spec -> ref
#
# Resolving it here means the source and the patch set are always read from ONE
# commit and can never come from different moments. The PKGBUILD used to build
# `#branch=ogc` instead -- a moving branch, on the reasoning that armada tracks
# the fork's tip -- and when the branch got far enough ahead of the last
# artifact, 9 of 17 patches stopped applying and every build failed until armada
# published again. Against the ref Terra pinned, that same patch set applies with
# zero fuzz: the branch was the stale half, not the patches.
SOURCE_REF_FROM=armada-terra-spec
SOURCE_REF_VAR=_ogcref
SOURCE_COMMIT_VAR=_ogccommit
SOURCE_GIT=https://github.com/OpenGamingCollective/gamescope.git

# Was TERRA.env at the old repo's root; packages/TERRA.env in the new one.
TERRA_ENV_FILE=packages/TERRA.env
TERRA_ENV_KEY=TERRA_COMMIT
TERRA_SPEC_REPO=terrapkg/packages
TERRA_SPEC_PATH=anda/games/terra-gamescope/terra-gamescope.spec
# Tried in order. Terra names the ref as `%global ver <tag>` today and used
# `%global gamescope_commit <sha>` before it; older commits still resolve
# through the second spelling, so both stay listed.
TERRA_SPEC_KEYS="ver gamescope_commit"
